  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 310 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonally warm temperatures and comfortable \nhumidity continue through Wednesday.\n\nLatest wx analysis shows an upper level trough across the Mid-\nAtlantic and Northeast regions with surface level high pressure over \nthe area. Pleasant conditions are noted with temperatures in the \nlower to mid 80s with dew points in the mid to upper 50s and mostly \nsunny skies. High pressure will remain over the area tonight and \nbegin pushing offshore Wednesday, which will allow pleasant \nconditions again tonight with lows in the lower 60s inland and upper \n60s along the coast (a few upper 50s may be observed in the \npiedmont). Temperatures will be a few degrees warmer tomorrow with \nhighs in the upper 80s, as the high pressure begins moving offshore \nshifting the winds out of the S. Dewpoints will increase as well, \nbut overall, humidity levels will remain tolerable for August.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled conditions return mid to late week \nwith daily chances for showers and thunderstorms.\n\nGlobal models indicate that surface high pressure settles off the \nSoutheast coast later Wednesday into Thursday and the flow aloft \nshifts back to the SW. A few showers/tstms could creep into western \nportions of the area late Wednesday evening. A slow moving front \napproaches the area Thursday bringing the best chance for \nshowers/storms on Thursday and Friday. The severe threat seems low \nat this time for both days from weaker upper flow (500 mb flow \naround 20-25 kt). Showers are possible in the mornings both days, \nwhich could further hinder the severe threat. However, if precip can \nhold off until the afternoon hours a few stronger tstms are \npossible, especially given that our local area will be under the RRQ \nof a strengthening jet core over New England. The main threat will \nbe locally heavy rain from showers/tstms. WPC maintains a Marginal \nRisk of excessive rainfall Thursday and Friday, but given the setup, \nthe showers/storms seem scattered by nature causing a lower \nconfidence in flooding. The consensus amongst the 12z/25 models \npushes the front S Saturday with the best shower/tstm chances across \nSE VA/NE NC, then trending drier Sunday/Monday with PoPs near to \nslightly below climo.\n\nSeasonally hot and humid Thursday through Sunday with high \ntemperatures primarily in the mid 80s, and potentially trending \nabove average into the upper 80s to lower 90s Sunday and Monday.",
